---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HumanWrittenMark from "../components/HumanWrittenMark.astro";

// The analytics disclosure, and the control that turns it off (P3, P4, P7).
//
// The design that produced this is docs/front-end-analytics-design.md. This
// page is the visitor-facing half of it: what is measured, who processes it,
// how long it is kept, and the one switch that stops it. Everything stated
// here is a promise the code has to keep — if the configuration in
// src/scripts/analytics.ts changes, this page changes with it.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy | Built by Woodley"
  description="What this site measures, what it does not, and how to turn it off."
>
  <main class="page-shell privacy-page">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <div>
        <h1 class="page-heading">Privacy.</h1>
      </div>
      <p class="page-subtitle">
        This site counts how it is read. It does not try to work out who is
        reading it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose privacy-prose">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        One anonymous identifier is stored in your browser, so that coming back
        tomorrow is not counted as a different person arriving. It belongs to
        this site only — no third-party cookies, nothing shared with anywhere
        else, and nothing attached to it that says who you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no consent banner. Instead: the switch further down deletes
        that identifier outright rather than merely muting it, and if your
        browser already asks not to be tracked, this site listens without you
        having to click anything.
      </p>

      <h2>What gets measured</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Which page was viewed, and how long it was open.</li>
        <li>How far down the page you scrolled, and which chapter of the landing page you reached.</li>
        <li>
          Which named controls were used — the navigation links, the contact
          buttons, the theme toggle, the photo viewer's controls, the map's
          Desktop/Mobile switch. A fixed list, chosen one at a time, not "every
          click".
        </li>
        <li>
          How fast the page loaded and rendered on your device, what its images
          cost to fetch, and how quickly the photo viewer responded.
        </li>
        <li>
          Whether this browser has been here before — as a count, not a history.
          "Forty people came back this month" is the question; "here is what
          you did last time" is not.
        </li>
        <li>
          Roughly where the visit came from: a country, sometimes a province or
          state. Derived from your IP address, which is then discarded — see
          below.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What does not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No fingerprint, and no profile.</strong> The identifier
          described above is a random value with nothing attached to it: no
          name, no account, no email, no list of what you looked at. It is
          readable only by this site, and if you delete it, it is gone — this
          site will not try to recognise you some other way, which is the
          whole difference between storing an identifier and fingerprinting
          you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No precise location.</strong> Country and region only. Not
          your city, not coordinates, and never your IP address itself — that
          is used to work out the country and then dropped at the door.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No page content.</strong> Link text, headings, class names and
          element structure are never sent. Neither is my email address, even
          when the event being recorded is that someone clicked the button
          holding it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No session recording, heatmaps, or replay.</strong> Nothing
          watches your pointer or reconstructs your visit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No advertising, no data sale, no cross-site tracking.</strong>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who processes it</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="https://posthog.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          PostHog
        </a>, on their US cloud, as the processor. Events reach them through
        this site's own domain rather than a third-party one — a small proxy on
        the path <code>/sawdust</code> — which means no request goes to a
        different origin than the one in your address bar.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your IP address and browser user-agent are sent to that proxy, as they
        are with any web request. PostHog uses the address for two things as the
        event arrives — working out an approximate country, and recognising
        traffic from datacentres so that scrapers are not counted as readers —
        and then discards it. The coarse location is kept; the number it came
        from is not, and neither of them ever reaches me. Event data is retained
        for one year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cloudflare, who serve this site, additionally count requests at their
        edge for basic traffic and performance figures. That is aggregate
        infrastructure data — request counts, countries, load timings — with no
        per-person dimension available to me at any tier.
      </p>

      <h2>Turning it off</h2>
      <p>
        If your browser sends <strong>Global Privacy Control</strong> or
        <strong>Do Not Track</strong>, it is already off: the analytics code is
        never even downloaded, let alone run. You do not have to do anything
        here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Otherwise, this switch decides. It takes effect immediately — no
        reload — and turning analytics <em>off</em> also deletes the identifier
        from this browser rather than leaving it sitting there unused. Your
        choice itself is remembered on this device under the key
        <code>analytics-opt-out</code>, so that the site does not start
        measuring again the next time you visit.
      </p>

      <div class="privacy-optout" data-analytics-optout hidden>
        <p class="privacy-optout-state" data-analytics-optout-state aria-live="polite"></p>
        <button type="button" class="button" data-analytics-optout-toggle></button>
      </div>

      <noscript>
        <p>
          With JavaScript off, no analytics run at all — the entire measurement
          layer is client-side.
        </p>
      </noscript>

      <h2>Why any of this exists</h2>
      <p>
        This is a portfolio with a long landing page, a photography archive, and
        a contact link that produces no pageview when it is used. Without
        measurement I have no way to know whether anyone reads past the first
        chapter, whether the archive is ever reached, or whether the site is
        slow on a phone. The design that decided what to collect — and, at more
        length, what not to — is
        <a
          href="https://github.com/mcwoodle/builtbywoodley-astro/blob/mainline/docs/front-end-analytics-design.md"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >in the repository</a>, along with everything else that runs this site.
      </p>

      <HumanWrittenMark />
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // The control renders only once this runs, because a switch that cannot work
  // is worse than no switch: with JavaScript off there is nothing to turn off,
  // and a dead button saying "analytics are on" would be a lie.
  import { isAvailable, isOptedOut, setOptedOut } from "../scripts/analytics";

  const COPY = {
    on: {
      state: "Analytics are on for this browser.",
      action: "Turn analytics off",
    },
    off: {
      state: "Analytics are off for this browser.",
      action: "Turn analytics back on",
    },
    unavailable: {
      state:
        "Analytics are off — either your browser asked for that, or this build has none.",
      action: "",
    },
  };

  function render() {
    const box = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-analytics-optout]");
    const state = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-analytics-optout-state]");
    const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(
      "[data-analytics-optout-toggle]",
    );
    if (!box || !state || !button) return;

    box.hidden = false;

    // GPC, DNT, or a build with analytics compiled out. The choice below would
    // change nothing, so it is reported rather than offered.
    if (!isAvailable()) {
      state.textContent = COPY.unavailable.state;
      button.hidden = true;
      return;
    }

    const copy = isOptedOut() ? COPY.off : COPY.on;
    state.textContent = copy.state;
    button.hidden = false;
    button.textContent = copy.action;
  }

  document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    const target = event.target;
    if (!(target instanceof Element)) return;
    if (!target.closest("[data-analytics-optout-toggle]")) return;
    setOptedOut(!isOptedOut());
    render();
  });

  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", render);
  render();
</script>

<style>
  .privacy-prose {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .privacy-optout {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 18%, transparent);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .privacy-optout-state {
    margin: 0;
    flex: 1 1 18ch;
  }
</style>
